import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Gate 3 — the form-level proposer (the fifth move reaches loop)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/gate3-form-level-proposer.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/gate3-form-level-proposer.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/gate3-form-level-proposer.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="gate-3-—-the-form-level-proposer-the-fifth-move-reaches-loop" tabindex="-1">Gate 3 — the form-level proposer (the fifth move reaches <code>loop</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#gate-3-—-the-form-level-proposer-the-fifth-move-reaches-loop" aria-label="Permalink to “Gate 3 — the form-level proposer (the fifth move reaches loop)”">​</a></h1><p><code>lab/bridge/form_propose.py</code>, exercised by <code>lab/bridge/combinator_probe.py --gate2 --extracted --form-propose</code> and pinned by <code>tests/test_form_propose.py</code>.</p><h2 id="result" tabindex="-1">Result <a class="header-anchor" href="#result" aria-label="Permalink to “Result”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Gate 3 is reached — with one named residual.</strong> On the real extracted winner (the 7-block mixed fold, <code>455,752</code>-FLOP greedy arm) the proposer reaches a program morphism containing <strong>two <code>Loop</code>-headed folds</strong>, one per chunk-width family, under <strong>one step schema</strong> — and the whole thing is <em>e-graph certified</em>: expanding the morphism (<code>comb_to_dag</code>) lands in the winner&#39;s own root e-class (class 25), and every expanded block lands in its own block e-class.</p><ul><li>chains found: blocks <code>[1,2,3]</code> (4-wide family, seed class 4081) and <code>[4,5,6]</code> (16-wide family, seed class 3549); block 0 is peeled.</li><li>per-block verification: all six covered blocks <code>True</code>.</li><li><code>unroll(step,3)</code> per chain → <code>saturate_comb</code> with <code>PROPOSE_LOOP</code> folds each to <code>Loop(step,3)</code> (<code>loop_propose</code> fired 2×; cost 91 each).</li><li>the assembled program <code>seq(pre, par(unroll, unroll, id), post)</code> reproduces the winner&#39;s concat skeleton and lands in the root class.</li><li>obstruction reported (not hidden): <code>state-chain-broken:block3-&gt;block4</code> — the single-loop arm carries the state across the boundary and the per-block checks fail at 4,5,6, because the saturation never merged the small-family terminal state with the large-family initial state. <s>the two spellings are the same <em>value</em></s> — <strong>corrected 2026-10-16</strong> (see below): measured directly they are <em>different</em> values, <code>carried = γ^12·M_4</code> to 1.8e-15, so the pair is not mergeable at all. Two loops remains the honest result — for a stronger reason than first recorded.</li></ul><h2 id="the-move-that-worked" tabindex="-1">The move that worked <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-move-that-worked" aria-label="Permalink to “The move that worked”">​</a></h2><p>Use the saturated e-graph <strong>as the anti-unification oracle</strong>, instead of either pattern-matching the winner&#39;s syntax (move 1: <code>unroll</code> never appears) or assuming the algebra (moves 2–4 each failed on the unfolded geometric sum / re-derived state). Extraction <em>chose</em> the unfolded spelling, but saturation had already <strong>derived</strong> the factored one: each block&#39;s state-read e-class contains a <code>matmul(q̃, M)</code> member whose right operand is the q-free accumulated matrix, and those M-classes are linked by <code>add(mul(w,·),B)</code> update members — the recurrence is in the graph, only not chosen.</p><p>So the step is <strong>synthesised</strong>: <code>pack(S′, o)</code> with <code>S′ = w·S + (k ⊙ η)ᵀ v</code>, <code>o = om_decayed(qkᵀ/c, D, v) + ξ ⊙ ((q/c) @ S)</code>, over 9 wires (state + own q/k/v splits + the five decay/scale constants). Everything family-varying — index, chunk width, constants — is on wires, so one schema serves both families. Wire bindings are derived per block and every binding must be <em>graph-resident</em> (<code>eg._locate</code>), so a wrong guess fails the check rather than fabricate.</p><h2 id="what-this-settles" tabindex="-1">What this settles <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-settles" aria-label="Permalink to “What this settles”">​</a></h2><ul><li>The combinator presentation <strong>can</strong> function as a search substrate: search + this proposer reaches <code>loop</code>-headed terms on the real extracted winner — twice, under one schema, certified.</li><li>The residual obstruction is precise — and, after re-measurement, <em>stronger</em> than first recorded. See the next section.</li><li>Lab-only, as required: no shipped IR node, no engine change; all work is in <code>lab/</code> + the proposal rule already in <code>lab/bridge/loop_propose.py</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="correction-—-the-boundary-is-a-value-mismatch-not-a-reach-gap" tabindex="-1">Correction — the boundary is a <em>value</em> mismatch, not a reach gap <a class="header-anchor" href="#correction-—-the-boundary-is-a-value-mismatch-not-a-reach-gap" aria-label="Permalink to “Correction — the boundary is a value mismatch, not a reach gap”">​</a></h2><p>Follow-up measurement (the <code>compensated_loop_check</code> arm now in <code>form_propose.py</code>, pinned by <code>tests/test_form_propose.py::test_boundary_residual_is_a_value_mismatch</code>):</p><ul><li>The earlier claim &quot;the two spellings are the same <em>value</em>&quot; was asserted, not measured — the numeric arm only ever compared the per-chain rebuilt concat. Lowered and compared directly, the carried boundary state (the 4-wide chain&#39;s terminal, e-class 9763) and the large family&#39;s initial state (class 3549, <code>matmul(transpose(mul(k_{(16,16,16,16):0}, η_16)), v_{(16,…):0})</code>) differ by exactly <strong>γ^12</strong> (<code>max|Δ| = 12.4</code>; <code>max|carried − γ^12·M_4| = 1.8e-15</code>). The fold invariant is <code>S_i = γ^{φ_i}·A_i</code> — the absolute accumulation <code>A_i = Σ_p γ^{−p} k_pv_p</code> rescaled by the start of the previous <em>own-partition</em> chunk; across the boundary <code>φ</code> jumps 8 → 0. <strong>No sound rewrite can merge the classes</strong> — this is not a budget or rule-coverage gap.</li><li>Budget arm for diligence: <code>iters=20</code>, budgets 2–3× (kway 600 / bridge 1200 / reassoc 1200 / chunkify 200) → <strong>69,993 enodes</strong>, and the boundary is unchanged: 3549-class singleton, no <code>add(mul(w,·),B)</code> link, <code>decay_vec(offset=4,t=16)</code> still absent.</li><li>The uniform step is still correct <em>at the value level</em> with two reindexed wires — <code>ξ&#39;_4 = decay_vec(offset=4, t=16)</code> makes block 4&#39;s output exact to 2.2e-16, and steering the update with <code>w&#39; = decay_vec(t=1, offset=4)</code> lands the block-5 state at exactly M_5 (1.8e-15). The compensated arm records verdicts <code>{1:T, 2:T, 3:T, 4:F, 5:F, 6:F}</code> and names the two concrete residuals: <ul><li><code>boundary-renorm-unbound:block3-&gt;block4</code> — the producing-step steering pair <code>decay_vec(t=1, offset=-8)</code> / <code>decay_vec(t=4, sign=-1, offset=-12)</code> is not in the graph (negative-offset decay vectors are never minted);</li><li><code>boundary-xi-nonresident:block4</code> — <code>decay_vec(offset=4, t=16)</code> is not in the graph, and even minted it could not help: class 3549 is a singleton and no staged rule splits a bare <code>matmul</code> along its contraction axis (only <code>om_decayed</code> has <code>chunkify_keys</code>), so the compensated read enode <code>mul(ξ&#39;_4, matmul(q̃_4, carried))</code> has no path into class 93.</li><li>at block 5 the residual <em>is</em> a pure reach gap: the steered state equals M_5 in value but lands in a fresh class (the e-graph never derived <code>γ^4·carried + B_4 ≡ M_5</code>).</li></ul></li><li>What would close it (measured, not guessed): a &quot;state-reindex&quot; law family — a contraction-axis bisection for bare <code>matmul</code>, a <code>decay_vec</code> block-split law producing signed offsets, and scalar slide/merge laws on <code>mul(decay_vec, ·)</code>, plus saturation budget for the derivation. None is a one-line rule; whether that bundle pays for itself is an ADR-0009 question, not a gate-3 oversight.</li><li>Separately observed: blocks 3 and 6 carry 8 / 10 factored state candidates that are all <em>the same value</em> (verified numerically, max|Δ| = 0 between all pairs) in unmerged e-classes — the reach-gap phenomenon is real, just not at the boundary.</li></ul><h2 id="close-—-the-state-reindex-law-family-lands-loop-step-6" tabindex="-1">Close — the state-reindex law family lands <code>Loop(step,6)</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#close-—-the-state-reindex-law-family-lands-loop-step-6" aria-label="Permalink to “Close — the state-reindex law family lands Loop(step,6)”">​</a></h2><p>The &quot;what would close it&quot; bundle above is now built and measured in <code>lab/bridge/state_reindex.py</code> (lab law family, not in <code>DEFAULT</code>):</p><ul><li><code>matmul_contract_split(sizes)</code> — the bare-<code>matmul</code> contraction-axis bisection as a law <em>parameter</em> (the <code>om_decayed_kway_blocks</code> precedent). On <code>M_4</code>&#39;s singleton 3549-class the measured <code>(4,4,4,4)</code> partition mints the four 4-chunk contributions — and through the naturality chain the boundary&#39;s signed-offset vectors.</li><li><code>split_mul_aligned</code> / <code>split_unsqueeze</code> / <code>decay_vec_split_*</code> — the chain that pushes the minted splits through <code>mul</code>, <code>unsqueeze</code> (with <code>split_transpose</code>, shipped) down to <code>decay_vec</code>, whose block-split produces <strong>signed</strong> offsets: <code>dv(g,T,-1,o)[start_i+k] = dv(g,SZ_i,-1,o-start_i)</code> — mints <code>dv(t=4,-1,-8)</code>, <code>dv(t=4,-1,-12)</code> and <code>decay_vec_usq_factor</code> mints the scalar <code>usq(dv(t=1,-8))</code> steering weight.</li><li><code>decay_scalar_gather</code> (<code>γ^a·(γ^b·X) = γ^{a+b}·X</code>), <code>decay_scalar_id*</code> (<code>γ^0·X = X</code>), <code>state_eta_slide*</code> (<code>γ^a ⊙ ((k⊙η_o)ᵀv) = (k⊙η_{o+a})ᵀv</code>), plus <code>distribute_mul_add</code> — the slide/merge that re-indexes <code>γ^{-8}·S_in3</code> into the absolute accumulation the bisection spells.</li></ul><p>The driver <code>reindex_close</code> measures the boundary (the missing steering eta&#39;s <code>t=4</code> gives the fine width, the target&#39;s contraction gives <code>T=16</code> → sizes <code>(4,4,4,4)</code>), runs one mint pass, then re-walks the compensated arm with a <code>post_add</code> saturation hook so the steered terms merge as they are minted. On the real winner: verdicts <code>{1..6: True}</code>, the steered <code>S&#39;_4</code> lands in class 3549 exactly (<code>add(mul(w_{-8},S_in3), B&#39;_3)</code> ≡ <code>add(add(m_0,m_1),m_2)+m_3</code>), and the emitted <strong>single <code>Loop(step,6)</code></strong> lowers into the winner&#39;s root e-class — <code>single_whole_equal: True</code> (<code>test_state_reindex_closes_boundary</code>).</p><p>Two implementation lessons, both measured:</p><ul><li>Budget accounting is in <em>enodes minted</em>, not fires, and persists across <code>eg.run</code> calls — the first close silently suspended <code>distribute_mul_add</code> at its 4000-enode budget before the steered merges could fire. <code>reindex_budgets(sizes)</code> now sizes the family budget to cover the mint pass plus every post-add round.</li><li>Post-mint the read classes hold extra factored members, so <code>seed_cands[0]</code> can be a same-shape/wrong-value candidate — the arm now probes the seed by verifying the first covered block.</li></ul><p><code>compensated_loop_check</code> also records <code>rep.single_envs</code> / <code>rep.single_seed</code> so the emitted loop&#39;s wire bundles are the <em>compensated</em> ones (steering <code>w&#39;</code>/<code>η&#39;</code> on the producing step, the reindexed <code>ξ&#39;</code> on the boundary read) — the step schema itself is unchanged, only the per-iteration wires differ.</p>`,20)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};